@page "/whats-coming"

<PageTitle>What's coming · louis-agent</PageTitle>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">What's coming</span>
    <h2 class="chrome">Planned, and said so.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Everything else on this site works today. This page is the plan: written on 2026-11-08 from the roadmap and the open list, each item by its id, in the order it's built. A test holds every item here to an open row of that list, so what ships leaves this page the day it does. None of it is a promise of a date.</p>
</section>

<section class="band plans">
    <span class="kicker">The framework's track, first</span>
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><span class="plan-id">F106</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>The framework's track.</strong> A builder for the common path, so your first agent is a few lines. The framework's own docs, from "your first agent in ten minutes" to writing a pack and an identity. A real-world workflow as the demo, an agent team that triages a service's errors and fixes them on a branch. And a measured side-by-side with Microsoft Agent Framework.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F39</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>The workflow, in the engine.</strong> The fourth concept: one delegation function, a hand-off of a whole conversation to a named peer, typed results checked in code, one policy per turn tree for depth, rounds and money.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F43</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Conversations that survive a restart.</strong> Saved to MongoDB so a person restarts one where it was, on another device too, and a tenant on every ledger row.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F74</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Packaging.</strong> The public surface declared, a reproducible build, a package and a consumer test built from it. Then the engine's own repository and NuGet.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F44</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>The engine as a product.</strong> Speed, metrics and the packages a team installs.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="band plans">
    <span class="kicker">Then, what a sale needs</span>
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><span class="plan-id">F7</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Cost you can see and stop.</strong> An estimate before a turn, budgets that warn, ask or stop, cost per customer, and the ledger checked against the bill.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F41</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Drafts a person approves.</strong> A tool declares its effect, approval follows the risk, and a draft is checked in code before anyone sees it.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F42</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Safety and audit.</strong> Untrusted content framed as data, an audit log, rate limits, and injection simulations as the red-team suite.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F36</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Their systems, no code.</strong> Someone else's MCP server plugged in as a pack, with our guards around its tools.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F8</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Reliability.</strong> Retries, a fallback model, timeouts.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F15</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Local models, properly.</strong> A small model given its whole prompt within its limits, and the limits known.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F46</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Localisation.</strong> Every text from a resource file beside its enum, so a site is configured into its language, not coded.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F40</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>The sales playground.</strong> Each pitch a simulation over made-up data, with its cost on screen, so a pitch is also a measurement.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="band plans">
    <span class="kicker">This site and the demos</span>
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><span class="plan-id">F107</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Sign in before the chat.</strong> A guest is sent to sign in before the coding agent's chat. The guide in the corner stays open to everyone, within a cap.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F108</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>The admin portal as a role.</strong> A signed-in person carries the admin role, and the admin key goes.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F48</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Puls's cook as a home cook and teacher.</strong> A shopping list with its prices, a pantry, and answers that teach.</li>
        <li><span class="plan-id">F50</span> <span class="planned">planned</span> <strong>Puls's local specialists.</strong> One at a time, each a role on the packs it names, with its eval run before it is shown.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="band-note">Open to everyone now: <a href="/proof">what's measured</a> · <a href="/architecture">what exists</a></p>
</section>
